From: Ubuntu iceland port <jstockdale@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: kaanapali-oneplus-iceland: drop panic=0 from bootargs

panic=0 on the command line is read twice: the kernel takes it as
"never reboot on panic" (identical to the default CONFIG_PANIC_TIMEOUT=0,
so nothing is lost by dropping it), but initramfs-tools' panic() ALSO
parses panic= and a numeric value means "reboot after N seconds instead
of spawning the rescue shell" - with N=0, an instant reboot on any
initramfs failure. Measured on the OnePlus Pad 4: a failed root mount
becomes a silent boot loop, and on a freshly flashed boot_b (slot
retries reset, successful cleared) each loop iteration burns an ABL
retry. An Ubuntu initramfs needs the argument gone; Perpetua's own
rescue init never parses it, so its boot flow is unchanged aside from
relying on the identical built-in panic timeout default.

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/kaanapali-oneplus-iceland.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/kaanapali-oneplus-iceland.dts
index e22aade66262..3d0c91d262cc 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/kaanapali-oneplus-iceland.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/kaanapali-oneplus-iceland.dts
@@ -39,7 +39,7 @@ aliases {
 	};
 
 	chosen {
-		bootargs = "rdinit=/rescue/mainline-init panic=0 loglevel=7 log_buf_len=2M bootconfig";
+		bootargs = "rdinit=/rescue/mainline-init loglevel=7 log_buf_len=2M bootconfig";
 
 		/* The bootloader ramdisk carries the rescue and boot userspace. */
 		fdt-handoff {
